Define AT_RECURSIVE in the open_tree polyfill

The polyfill for a libc without the open_tree wrapper defines
OPEN_TREE_CLONE and OPEN_TREE_CLOEXEC, but the call site also uses
AT_RECURSIVE. glibc 2.28's <fcntl.h> does not define AT_RECURSIVE, so
libstore does not compile against it:

  linux-derivation-builder.cc:285:57: error: 'AT_RECURSIVE' was not
  declared in this scope

0x8000 is the kernel's value, from <linux/fcntl.h>. Where the libc
defines the name, the #ifndef leaves it alone, so the object code does
not change.

huggorm builds its manylinux_2_28 wheels in PyPA's image, which has
glibc 2.28 (huggorm#108). Nix 2.34 does not call open_tree here.

--- a/src/libstore/linux/build/linux-derivation-builder.cc
+++ b/src/libstore/linux/build/linux-derivation-builder.cc
@@ -50,6 +50,10 @@
 #    define OPEN_TREE_CLOEXEC O_CLOEXEC
 #  endif
 
+#  ifndef AT_RECURSIVE
+#    define AT_RECURSIVE 0x8000
+#  endif
+
 static int open_tree(int dirfd, const char * filename, unsigned int flags)
 {
     return ::syscall(__NR_open_tree, dirfd, filename, flags);
